his rule is very important for SSC/CGL error spotting.

WHO → Subject
WHOM → Object

The easiest way to understand it is exactly the method we've been using: identify the subject and object of the verb.


1. WHO = Subject

Use who when the pronoun is doing the action.

Example:

Marie Curie was a scientist who won two Nobel Prizes. ✅

Look at the relative clause:

who won two Nobel Prizes.

Ask:

Who won? → Marie Curie.

So who = subject.

Therefore:

Marie Curie was a scientist who won two Nobel Prizes. ✅

❌ ...a scientist whom won...

Because whom cannot be the subject in this traditional rule.



2. WHOM = Object

Use whom when the pronoun receives the action.

The person whom I met yesterday is my friend. ✅

Look at:

I met whom yesterday?

Answer → the person.

So:

I = subject
met = verb
whom = object

Therefore:

The person whom I met yesterday is my friend. ✅



⭐ Your “WHO/WHOM” shortcut

Use the HE/HIM test:

If you can replace it with HE → WHO

___ helped me.

He helped me. ✅

Therefore:

Who helped me? ✅

If you can replace it with HIM → WHOM

I helped ___.

I helped him. ✅

Therefore:

Whom did I help? ✅

Memorize:

WHO = HE
WHOM = HIM


3. Example: “The person who/whom helped him”

The person who helped him is my old friend. ✅

Break the relative clause:

who helped him

Ask:

Who helped him?

→ The person

So who = subject.

4. “I will visit my parents who/whom lives in Delhi.”

The correct answer according to the video's traditional rule is:

I will visit my parents who live in Delhi. ✅

Why?

Within the relative clause:

who live in Delhi

Who lives in Delhi?

→ my parents

So who = subject.

Also notice:

parents → plural → live, not lives.

So there are actually two things to check:

who → subject
parents → plural → live

5. “He is not one of those who/whom will help everyone.”

Correct:

He is not one of those who will help everyone he meets. ✅

Why?

Look at:

who will help everyone...

Ask:

Who will help everyone?

→ those people

So who = subject.


6. After a preposition → WHOM

This is one of the easiest rules.

The person to whom I am speaking is my old friend. ✅

Here:

to + whom

whom is the object of the preposition to.

Similarly:

With whom did you travel? ✅

Not:

❌ With who did you travel?

Traditional exam grammar:

Preposition + whom

Examples:

to whom
with whom
by whom
for whom
from whom

7. “By whom was the Ramayana written?”

Correct:

By whom was the Ramayana written? ✅

Why whom?

Because:

by + whom

Here whom is the object of the preposition by.

It is also the person who performed the action in the passive construction.

Compare:

Who wrote the Ramayana?
→ who = subject

By whom was the Ramayana written?
→ whom = object of by

8. Very important comparison
Sentence 1:

The person who met me yesterday is my friend. ✅

Ask:

Who met me?

→ The person.

Therefore:

who = subject

Sentence 2:

The person whom I met yesterday is my friend. ✅

Ask:

I met whom?

→ The person.

Therefore:

whom = object

Notice the difference:

who met me

vs.

I met whom

This is the BEST way to understand the difference.


9. “He is the person who/whom everyone thought has stolen the purse.”

This looks complicated, but break it into clauses.

He is the person who/whom everyone thought has stolen the purse.

The underlying idea is:

Everyone thought he had stolen the purse.

Here the relative pronoun represents the person who stole the purse.

So:

who has stolen the purse. ✅

Because who is the subject of has stolen.

Don't get confused by everyone thought.


10. “He identified a girl who/whom we found to be the main culprit.”

Correct:

He identified a girl whom we found to be the main culprit. ✅

Look at the clause:

we found whom to be the main culprit

Ask:

We found whom to be the main culprit?

→ the girl.

So whom = object of found.

11. “He is the person who/whom we appreciate for his work.”

Correct:

He is the person whom we appreciate for his work. ✅

Why?

We appreciate whom?

→ the person.

Therefore:

whom = object
